        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="email" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Email recipients&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:#5c5c5c&quot;&gt;Alert and budget emails&lt;/span&gt;" style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;verticalAlign=top;arcSize=12;fillColor=#f7f6f4;strokeColor=#8a8781;dashed=1;dashPattern=5 4;spacingTop=37;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="890" y="1030" width="200" height="80" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="email-icon" parent="email" value="" style="shape=image;html=1;aspect=fixed;imageAspect=0;image=data:image/svg+xml,;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="88.0" y="11" width="24" height="24" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-health" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Secrets and data access: what reads what, and why&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Probes:&lt;/b&gt; GET /healthz on port 8000, sent by the Container Apps platform from inside the environment, as both the liveness and the readiness probe. Excluded from both sign-in layers.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;One identity, three roles:&lt;/b&gt; the container app&amp;#x27;s user-assigned managed identity holds AcrPull on the registry, Key Vault Secrets User on the vault, and Storage Blob Data Contributor on the storage account. No password, key or connection string is ever hard-coded.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Database credential:&lt;/b&gt; one PostgreSQL administrator login, generated by Terraform and stored in Key Vault; the app reads it as a Key Vault-referenced container app secret at start-up, over the same private network path.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Two sign-in layers:&lt;/b&gt; Entra (network-position trust, before the app sees a request) then the app&amp;#x27;s own username/password session (application roles: admin/user).&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Alerts:&lt;/b&gt; container restarts, 5xx bursts, PostgreSQL CPU above threshold, and blob storage capacity above threshold, all to the Action Group.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Does not prove:&lt;/b&gt; that users can reach the app over the hub and corporate DNS, or that Terraform&amp;#x27;s own first-apply write to Key Vault succeeded (it needs either a network path into the spoke or a temporary public allow-list)." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="1130" width="800" height="260" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-plain" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;How users reach the app, and where their data lives&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;What happens&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;• A user opens the app&amp;#x27;s internal address from the corporate network, never over the internet&lt;br&gt;• Microsoft Entra ID checks they belong to an allowed group, then the app itself asks for a username and password&lt;br&gt;• Everything the user creates in the app is saved to a private database; any images they upload go to private file storage&lt;br&gt;• The app never stores its own passwords in plain text, and its own secrets live in a vault, not in its code&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why we do it this way&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;• Two sign-ins because the app needs its own accounts and roles (admin vs. regular user), on top of Entra keeping outsiders out&lt;br&gt;• The database and file storage have no public address at all, so they cannot be reached except through the app&lt;br&gt;• One identity for the app means nobody has to remember or rotate a database password by hand" style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="860" y="1130" width="800" height="260" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-mind" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;What to keep in mind&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hub dependency:&lt;/b&gt; users reach the app only once the platform team completes the hub side (peering, routing, corporate DNS). Agree it before the first apply.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Single database credential:&lt;/b&gt; Terraform and the app share one PostgreSQL administrator login. Add a scoped role if a second consumer of the database ever appears.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Key Vault&amp;#x27;s first-apply reach:&lt;/b&gt; the vault is private-endpoint-only, but Terraform must reach its data plane once to write the generated secrets.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Zones and HA:&lt;/b&gt; uat and prod run zone-redundant Container Apps replicas and zone-redundant PostgreSQL HA; dev and qa run neither. Both are fixed at creation.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Cost:&lt;/b&gt; about $70-78 a month in dev/qa and $400-410 in uat/prod, mostly idle, at West Europe list prices. Zone-redundant PostgreSQL HA (two General Purpose instances instead of one Burstable) drives most of the jump." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="1410" width="800" height="310" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-why" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Why pick this design, and its trade-offs&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;App login, not Entra-only:&lt;/b&gt; the app needs its own credentials and roles (admin/user), not just network-position trust. Trade-off: two sign-ins, and the app owns a password database it must hash and lock out correctly.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;One database credential, not two:&lt;/b&gt; avoids a second Terraform provider, and the network path it would need, just to create a more restricted role. Trade-off: not least-privilege, acceptable because nothing else connects to this server.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Key Vault, not plain container app secrets:&lt;/b&gt; nothing sensitive sits in tfvars or the image; rotation is a re-apply. Trade-off: Terraform&amp;#x27;s own first write needs a network path or a temporary public allow-list.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Managed identity, not a storage account key:&lt;/b&gt; no key to leak or rotate for images. Trade-off: the role assignment is scoped to the whole account, not just the images container.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;PostgreSQL HA in uat/prod only:&lt;/b&gt; rehearses and covers a zone failure where it matters. Trade-off: needs a non-Burstable SKU, doubling compute cost while it&amp;#x27;s on." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="860" y="1410" width="800" height="310" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="e0" parent="1" value="HTTPS" style="html=1;rounded=0;edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;endFill=1;strokeColor=#3f3f52;strokeWidth=1;fontSize=10;fontColor=#1c1c1c;labelBackgroundColor=#ffffff;endArrow=classic;endSize=6;jettySize=auto;exitX=1;exitY=0.5;exitPerimeter=0;entryX=0.85;entryY=0;entryPerimeter=0;" edge="1" source="user" target="hub">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="e1" parent="1" value="Peering" style="html=1;rounded=0;edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;endFill=1;strokeColor=#3f3f52;strokeWidth=1;fontSize=10;fontColor=#1c1c1c;labelBackgroundColor=#ffffff;endArrow=classic;endSize=6;jettySize=auto;" edge="1" source="hub" target="cae">
